    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d792">

                <pb facs="1062" n="938" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg938"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890g.6363/171a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Special Mission at Lausanne</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-12-28T19:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 28,
                                1922—7 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>68. Your attention is invited to the correspondence concerning the Open Door
                    policy with respect to a mandate for Mesopotamia and <pb facs="1063" n="939"
                        xml:id="pg939"/> to the instruction sent to our Embassy in Great Britain,
                    no. 233 of November 4, 1921,<anchor n="54" xml:id="d792-f1"/> with which was
                    enclosed a communication regarding the Turkish Petroleum Company to be presented
                    to the British Government. You will note that it is suggested in that
                    instruction that if claims under the Turkish Petroleum Company’s concession
                    continue to be asserted, the matter should be submitted to suitable
                    arbitration.</p>
                <p>In any general settlement with respect to possible claims the Department feels
                    that it would be advisable to provide for a suitable and impartial court or some
                    other appropriate plan for arbitration so that there may be a just settlement of
                    any contested claims in any of the territories under consideration. Such a plan
                    would include conflicting oil claims whether they arise from concessions the
                    extent or validity of which are in dispute or from conflicting property claims
                    such as are involved in connection with the claims of Abdul Hamid’s heirs, as
                    well as any other claims which may cause difficulty.</p>
                <p>The position of the American Government as stated above should be indicated upon
                    the proper occasion.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
